{
  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: 3 days of profuse watery diarrhoea\n- Symptoms: watery stools nearly every hour while awake; crampy lower/central abdominal pain, intermittent, pain severity ~6/10 when present; nausea with at least one vomiting episode after milk; subjective fever/hot sensations (no measured temperature); decreased urinary frequency; feels fluids pass rapidly through (poor stool form); onset ~3 days ago; not improving.\n- Past medical history: Patient reports otherwise fit and well; no chronic medical conditions recalled.\n- Medications / Allergies: No regular medications. Taking paracetamol PRN. No known drug allergies.\n- Family / Social history: Father with stage 3 colon cancer. Brother recently had a similar diarrhoeal illness for ~5 days and is improving. Lives with mother and sister. Works in an office. Social smoker and occasional/heavy alcohol use 1–2 times/month.\n- Vital signs / exam findings: No objective vitals recorded. No physical exam documented (telephone/telehealth visit). Patient reports feeling hot and less frequent urine (possible dehydration).\n- Labs / imaging: None mentioned or performed.",
  "step2_vindicate": {
    "V": [
      "Mesenteric ischemia: Unlikely but can present with severe abdominal pain and diarrhoea; less likely in a young, otherwise healthy 27-year-old with intermittent cramping and high stool frequency and no risk factors."
    ],
    "I": [
      "Viral gastroenteritis (e.g., norovirus, rotavirus): Common cause of acute watery diarrhoea with nausea, vomiting, crampy abdominal pain, short 1–3 day course — consistent with rapid onset, high stool frequency, and similar illness in a household contact (brother).",
      "Bacterial foodborne gastroenteritis (e.g., enterotoxigenic E. coli, Salmonella, Campylobacter): Can cause profuse watery diarrhoea, cramping and fever; possible if exposed to contaminated food or shared exposure with brother.",
      "Foodborne toxin (Staphylococcal/Bacillus cereus): Causes rapid-onset vomiting and/or diarrhoea after contaminated food — vomiting after milk could reflect ingested trigger, though predominant diarrhoea favors other enteric infections."
    ],
    "N": [
      "Colorectal neoplasm: Family history of colon cancer is relevant for long-term risk, but new acute profuse watery diarrhoea in a 27-year-old is unlikely due to malignancy."
    ],
    "D": [
      "Secondary lactose intolerance (post-infectious): Acute gastroenteritis can transiently cause lactase deficiency leading to bloating, cramping and vomiting after milk — fits vomiting with milk and loose stools.",
      "Chronic malabsorption (less likely acute): e.g., celiac disease typically produces chronic symptoms rather than an acute 3-day illness."
    ],
    "I2": [
      "Medication-induced diarrhoea (e.g., antibiotics, laxatives): Patient reports no regular medications or recent antibiotics, so iatrogenic cause is unlikely.",
      "Alcohol/toxin ingestion: Heavy episodic alcohol or ingested toxin could cause GI upset, but course and household contact make infectious cause more likely."
    ],
    "C": [
      "Congenital GI conditions (e.g., congenital bile salt malabsorption): Unlikely to present acutely in adulthood with this pattern; not suggested by history."
    ],
    "A": [
      "Food allergy (e.g., IgE-mediated to milk): IgE food allergy typically causes immediate vomiting, urticaria or anaphylaxis; isolated delayed watery diarrhoea without other allergic signs is less consistent."
    ],
    "T": [
      "Traumatic/obstructive causes: No history of abdominal trauma or signs of obstruction; presentation favors diarrhoeal illness rather than trauma."
    ],
    "E": [
      "Hyperthyroidism: Can cause chronic diarrhoea and heat intolerance, but acute 3-day profuse watery diarrhoea with vomiting and household case favors infectious gastroenteritis.",
      "Electrolyte disturbance / dehydration: Consequence of high stool output rather than primary cause — relevant as a complication (reduced urine frequency, feeling hot)."
    ]
  },
